# Findings

## Current State Across Six Dimensions

The assessment scored TechnoVision across six dimensions on a 1–10 scale,
producing an overall readiness score of 6.2 — a solid foundation with two
areas that clearly lag the rest. Technology scored lowest at 4, reflecting
a legacy architecture that is stable but poorly integrated; customer
experience scored 5, reflecting a relationship-driven business that has
not yet made the shift to digital-first engagement that its own customers
are asking for.

| Assessment Area | Strength Score (1-10) | Key Strengths | Primary Challenges |
|-------------------|--------------------------|-------------------|---------------------|
| Market Position | 7 | Brand recognition, customer loyalty | Limited differentiation |
| Financial Health | 8 | Strong cash flow, growth trajectory | Cost structure optimization |
| Technology | 4 | Stable core systems | Legacy architecture, integration |
| Operations | 6 | Efficient processes | Manual workflows, scalability |
| Team & Culture | 7 | Committed leadership, talented team | Change readiness, skills gaps |
| Customer Experience | 5 | Relationship strength | Digital transformation needed |

## Market Opportunity

TechnoVision currently holds roughly 10% share of a $45M core market
growing at 15% annually — a healthy but not exceptional organic
opportunity on its own. What changes the calculus is the $55M of adjacent
and vertical opportunity the assessment identified: an estimated 60%
expansion potential in adjacent markets the company can serve with its
existing capabilities, plus a further 30% of revenue reachable in new
verticals once the platform is adapted. Taken together, the assessment
puts the total addressable opportunity above $100M.

